# Agent Discipline

## Think Before Coding

- State assumptions out loud before implementing.
- If the request is ambiguous, ask — do not silently pick an interpretation.
- If a simpler approach exists, push back and propose it.
- When confused, stop. Name what is unclear instead of guessing and running.

## Simplicity First

- Write the minimum code that solves the problem.
- No speculative abstractions or flexibility nobody asked for.
- Test: would a senior engineer call this overcomplicated? If yes, simplify.

## Surgical Changes

- Touch only what the task requires.
- Do not improve neighboring code or refactor what is not broken.
- Every changed line should trace back to the request.

## Goal-Driven Execution

- Turn vague instructions into verifiable targets before writing code.
- Example: "Add validation" → write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass.
- Define done in terms of observable outcomes (tests pass, behavior verified), not effort spent.
